Core principle: CONNECT THE PHYSICAL COMPONENT TO THE INFORMATION THAT ALREADY DESCRIBES IT.

Document purpose and status
This document consolidates the existing PITN / ROBOPARTS architecture into a manufacturer-facing implementation reference and peer-review package. It is based on the public ROBOPARTS repository materials, the supplied 34-TLD dashboard extract and Web3 domain examples, and the public ROBOPARTS marketplace page. It does not represent an independent security audit, a production deployment certification, or proof that every proposed API, integration, cryptographic mechanism, or lifecycle workflow is already implemented.
The objective is to establish what is demonstrated, what is specified, what a manufacturer can implement using its own systems, and what remains subject to reproducible testing. The architecture is intended to be deployable alongside existing ERP, PLM, MES, CAD/PDM, supplier, inventory and maintenance systems. It is not a requirement to replace those systems or transfer confidential source records to PITN.
Evidence-status vocabulary
Status
Meaning
Permitted claim
Demonstrated interface
A public page or interactive demonstration visibly presents a workflow or data relationship.
The interface demonstrates the presented concept/workflow; do not infer backend persistence or security.
Documented specification
A repository document defines a model, design, contract or target architecture.
The design is documented; implementation is not implied.
Code artifact
Code or a code example is present in a repository.
Source artifact exists; execution and deployment require separate evidence.
Implemented
A feature is present in an executable, deployed implementation and its version/configuration is identified.
Implementation exists in the identified environment.
Tested
A reproducible test with inputs, expected results and observed results has passed.
The tested behavior passed under stated conditions.
Independently verified
A qualified independent reviewer or test laboratory confirms the stated result.
Independent verification is limited to the test scope and evidence.


Do not collapse these statuses. A domain registration is not proof of a component registry; a passport UI is not proof of a cryptographic signature; a proposed API is not proof of an operating service; a blockchain transaction is not proof that the underlying physical-world claim is true.
1. Executive architecture statement
PITN is a blockchain-based namespace and persistent-identity architecture for physical manufacturing assets. ROBOPARTS applies that foundation to robotics components, assemblies, manufacturer identifiers, product revisions, serialized physical instances, BOM positions, compatibility, evidence and lifecycle relationships.
The architecture separates (1) the classification namespace, (2) the persistent identity reference, (3) the manufacturer's product definition, (4) the individual physical instance, (5) the assembly/BOM relationship, and (6) evidence and lifecycle events. These are linked but are not interchangeable.
Physical component → namespace classification → persistent identity → manufacturer/product record
→ serialized instance → BOM / installed configuration → evidence & lifecycle events
→ robot / fleet / service / sourcing applications
Manufacturer deployment principle
A manufacturer retains its existing systems of record and identifiers. PITN supplies a shared identity and relationship layer. Connectors or controlled exports map manufacturer records into PITN-compatible references; the manufacturer's ERP/PLM/MES remains authoritative for its source data. A deployment can begin with a read-only identity crosswalk and expand to controlled write-back only where governance permits.
Architectural boundaries
Namespace is a classification/identity context; it does not itself describe every attribute of a part.
Manufacturer part number identifies a manufacturer's product definition; a distributor SKU is issuer-specific; a serial number identifies an individual unit; a BOM position identifies a place in a configuration.
A robot identity persists across component replacement. A replaced component keeps its own identity and historical installation record.
Intended engineering BOM, as-built configuration and as-maintained configuration must be separately represented.
Blockchain may anchor namespace references, record digests, signatures or event commitments. Private commercial and personal information should remain off-chain.
AI-generated summaries and compatibility suggestions must remain distinguishable from manufacturer declarations, measured results and authorized engineering approvals.
