Domain and namespace evidence supplied for this assessment
The user supplied eight Web3 domain entries marked Polygon · Lifetime: st-rz-004-0002-yt1p.endeffector; factory01.humanoids; sentry02.cobots; humanoid.actuator; humanoid.encoder; humanoid.fastener; humanoid.endeffector; humanoid.controlsystem. Six map to the 34 robotics TLD schedule (.endeffector, .actuator, .encoder, .fastener and .controlsystem); .humanoids and .cobots are outside that 34-TLD schedule.
The user supplied a namespace-management dashboard extract containing 34 matching robotics TLDs. All 34 entries show reseller active. Twenty-seven show royalties active and seven show royalties not active. The dashboard reports registered-domain counts for .actuator (1), .encoder (1), .fastener (1), .controlsystem (1), .endeffector (2), and .humanoidro (2). Of these, five TLDs belong to the 34-TLD robotics schedule and account for six domains (.actuator 1, .encoder 1, .fastener 1, .controlsystem 1 and .endeffector 2); .humanoidro is outside that 34-TLD schedule. The separately supplied eight named domain examples include six domains within the 34-TLD set and two outside it (.humanoids and .cobots). These counts are dashboard observations and should be reconciled against a dated export and individual registration records before publication.
Commercial evidence: reseller-active and royalty-active controls evidence a configured monetization mechanism. They do not establish realized revenue. Revenue claims require dated sales, royalty accrual, payment or settlement records. No revenue amount is asserted here.
Blockchain verification: supplied registration text identifies Polygon and lifetime terms. For independent chain verification, append the domain registrar/contract record, chain ID, token or registry reference, transaction hash, resolver output and block timestamp. Do not describe the domains as independently verified on-chain until those records have been checked.
3. Canonical identity model — implementation contract
The following is the recommended canonical model, reconciling the existing ROBOPARTS data model and identity/BOM documents. It is an implementation-ready contract proposal (Schema v1.0.0), not a claim that all fields are already implemented in a production registry.
3.1 Entity separation
Entity
What it identifies
Key rule
Namespace
Classification context such as .actuator.
Versioned registry entry; namespace name is not a product or physical instance.
Organization
Manufacturer, supplier, distributor, integrator, operator or service provider.
Organization identity and verification status are separate from a product record.
Product definition
Manufacturer-defined product/model/part and revision.
MPN + manufacturer + revision identifies the defined product context; preserve issuer and source.
Physical instance
One manufactured or serialized unit.
Serial/lot/physical identifier is not substituted for product MPN.
Robot/system
A machine or product-level system.
Stable robot identity persists when components are replaced.
Assembly/subsystem
A physical grouping within a system.
Hierarchical, typed relationships; do not infer hierarchy from names.
BOM/configuration
An intended, approved, as-built or as-maintained bill of materials.
Versioned and effective-dated; preserve prior revisions.
Installation relationship
A physical instance installed at a defined BOM position.
Removal closes an installation interval; replacement creates a new relationship.
Lifecycle event
An event affecting an identity or configuration.
Append-only history; event actor, time, evidence and status are captured.
Evidence reference
A document, measurement, source record, photo, certificate or transaction.
Store URI, issuer, digest, issue date and review status.
Compatibility assessment
A scoped assessment between a product/instance and a target system.
Unknown is not compatible; preserve missing evidence and limitations.


3.2 Canonical identifier rules
Assign every PITN-managed entity a globally unique immutable internal identifier (UUID). Recommended canonical reference form: urn:pitn:<entity-type>:<UUID>.
Keep human-readable labels and namespace-qualified Web3 domains as resolvable aliases/references; do not make mutable MPN, SKU, serial or BOM position the sole database primary key.
Namespace TLD values are normalized to lowercase ASCII where possible; internationalized namespace labels require a documented IDNA/Unicode normalization policy and collision review.
Preserve source-system identifiers verbatim, together with issuer/system, namespace, region (where relevant), and validity period.
Never silently recycle an identifier. Retirement, suspension and revocation are states, not deletion.
Do not assume a namespace-qualified name is a W3C DID or a URI resolver unless a specific resolution method and contract are defined and tested.
3.3 Required product and physical-instance fields
Field group
Required / recommended fields
Identity envelope
schema_version; record_type; record_id; canonical_id; namespace_tld; identity status; optional Web3 domain, chain, chain_id, registry/token reference, tx hash, resolver URI.
Classification
class name; taxonomy version; category/subclass.
Manufacturer
organization ID; legal/display name; authoritative URI; identity status; country code where applicable.
Product definition
manufacturer_part_number; revision; optional product/family/model IDs; revision effective dates; issuer-specific distributor SKUs.
Physical instance
instance ID; serial number (nullable if non-serialized); lot number; manufacturing date; physical identifier type/value; instance status.
Technical data
named attributes with value, unit, source status, method, source reference and observation time; typed mechanical/electrical/communication/software/environmental interfaces.
BOM relationships
relationship ID/type; target ID; BOM ID/revision; line ID; position; quantity; effective interval; approval/installation status; source reference.
Provenance
record source, source URI/record ID, recorded by/at, last update, evidence references and content digests.
Verification
overall status plus claim-level status, method, evidence references, verifier and verification timestamp.
Lifecycle
event ID/type/time/actor/status; location; configuration reference; evidence; prior event reference and event digest.


3.4 Core relationship rules
A Product definition may have many Physical instances; an instance must reference one product definition unless an explicitly modelled unknown/unidentified state is used.
A Robot/system may have many configurations. Each configuration references a versioned BOM.
A BOM contains line items that describe required/optional product definitions and quantities; actual installations reference physical instances.
An installation relationship is time-bounded. A removal closes the interval; it does not erase the historical relationship.
A replacement event links the removed instance, replacement instance, BOM position, approving authority and supporting compatibility evidence.
Lifecycle events refer to stable entity IDs and retain the actor, event time, record time, evidence and status.
Compatibility is a scoped assessment, not a boolean inferred from matching category names. Missing specifications remain unknown.
Every derived or measured technical attribute records its units, source type and provenance.
3.5 Example identity relationship
Robot: urn:pitn:robot:<UUID>
  └─ Configuration: CFG-2026-REV-A
      └─ BOM: HUMANOIDO-BOM-REV-A
          └─ Position: LEFT-ARM/ELBOW/ACTUATOR-01
              ├─ Required product: Manufacturer MPN ACT-4721, Rev A
              └─ Installed physical instance:
                   urn:pitn:component:<UUID>
                   serial: SN-100238
                   namespace: .actuator
                   web3 alias: humanoid.actuator (if assigned)
                   status: installed
                   evidence: receiving + inspection + installation records
The domain alias in the example is illustrative. The supplied registration `humanoid.actuator` is evidence of a named domain registration as reported by the user; the example does not establish that the registered domain currently resolves to the component record shown.
