The 34 namespace definitions and dashboard statuses are also provided as a companion CSV. The seven added names are recorded as additions relative to the historical 27. Because the dashboard extract shows seven royalty-inactive TLDs, the current status should be read directly from the supplied dashboard, not inferred from whether a namespace is an addition.
Crosswalk interpretation
The original 27 form the documented ROBOPARTS physical BOM taxonomy. The expanded 34 portfolio adds electrical connector, rolling bearing, circuit board, robotic joint, power distribution, safety controller and kinematic link classifications. These additions increase classification granularity; they do not require a change to the persistent identity principles.
Namespace governance requirements
Maintain a registry record per TLD: canonical spelling, display label, definition, layer, parent/related namespaces, owner/controller, registry authority, chain/network, registry contract, activation state, royalty state, reseller state and effective dates.
Maintain version history with created/modified/deprecated dates, author, rationale and supersession relationships.
Do not merge similarly named namespaces (e.g. .endeffector and .endeffectors) without explicit governance and compatibility policy.
Record domain registrations as individual records beneath a namespace; never infer that a TLD's dashboard presence means a domain has been registered.
Publish an auditable dated export and attach chain proof for claims of on-chain registration.
5. Manufacturer network implementation architecture
The manufacturer can implement PITN identity in its own network using a logical integration layer. A minimal deployment need not centralize all manufacturer data. It can begin with a controlled crosswalk table and read-only resolver service.
