6. API and data exchange contract
The existing API_AND_DATABASE_PROTOTYPE.txt defines FastAPI/SQLite as a proposed initial stack and lists core entities and endpoint concepts. It is explicitly marked UNTESTED. The following endpoint groups are a recommended contract boundary; exact route names, payloads and security policies must be frozen in an OpenAPI specification before implementation.
Resource group
Representative operations
Expected behavior
Namespaces
GET /v1/namespaces; GET /v1/namespaces/{tld}
Return definition, version, state, registry evidence references and related classes.
Identity
POST /v1/identities; GET /v1/identities/{canonical_id}
Create or resolve canonical identity; enforce uniqueness and authorization.
Products
POST/GET /v1/products; GET /v1/products/{id}
Maintain manufacturer product definitions and revisions.
Instances
POST /v1/instances; GET /v1/instances/{id}; GET /v1/instances/{id}/history
Maintain physical instances and serial/lot references.
Robots/configurations
POST /v1/robots; POST /v1/robots/{id}/configurations
Maintain persistent system identity and versioned configurations.
BOM
POST /v1/boms; GET /v1/boms/{id}; POST /v1/boms/{id}/validate
Maintain versioned BOM and typed line items.
Installations
POST /v1/installations; POST /v1/installations/{id}/remove
Time-bound physical instance placement; preserve history.
Events
POST /v1/events; GET /v1/identities/{id}/events
Append lifecycle events with actor, timestamp, evidence and idempotency.
Evidence
POST /v1/evidence; GET /v1/evidence/{id}
Register metadata, digest, issuer, review status; protect source content.
Verification
POST /v1/verifications; GET /v1/identities/{id}/verification
Record claim-level verification with methods and evidence.
Compatibility
POST /v1/compatibility-assessments; GET /v1/products/{id}/compatibility
Return scoped status, checked requirements, missing evidence and limitations.
Anchoring
POST /v1/anchors; GET /v1/anchors/{id}; POST /v1/anchors/{id}/verify
Anchor digest/reference and verify transaction/record consistency; separate from physical authenticity.


API conformance requirements
Version routes and payload schemas (e.g. /v1); return schema_version in every exchange.
Use stable UUID/URN identifiers; never use display name as a primary key.
Support idempotency keys for create/event operations and return conflict on duplicate immutable identifiers.
Use UTC RFC 3339 timestamps; distinguish event occurrence time from record ingestion time.
Return explicit validation errors with field paths; reject unknown enum values and malformed identifiers.
Support pagination, filtering, sorting and bulk import/export with reconciliation summaries.
Use OpenAPI 3.1 and publish machine-readable schemas and examples.
Define data ownership, retention, correction, deletion, export and dispute policies.
7. Cryptographic identity, provenance and blockchain verification
PITN's namespace architecture is blockchain-based by design. To convert the documented architecture and reported domain registrations into independently testable cryptographic evidence, the implementation must define and test the registry contract, resolution method, record digest, signer identity, signature envelope, event chain and revocation procedure.
Recommended trust model
Canonical record: serialize a record using a deterministic canonicalization profile (recommend RFC 8785 JSON Canonicalization Scheme or another explicitly selected profile).
Digest: compute SHA-256 over the canonical bytes; store algorithm and digest with the record version.
Signer: sign a domain-separated message containing record ID, schema version, digest, issuer ID, issued-at time and intended audience/context.
Key management: use organization-controlled keys in a managed KMS/HSM where appropriate; define rotation, backup, compromise and revocation procedures.
Anchor: submit only the minimum public identifier or digest/commitment required to the selected blockchain registry; keep sensitive data off-chain.
Verify: independently recompute digest, validate signature against trusted key history, verify signer authorization at signing time, and query chain state/transaction receipt.
Revoke/supersede: append a new signed status event; do not overwrite prior signed records or silently delete history.
Cryptographic acceptance tests
Test ID
Test procedure
Pass condition
CRYPTO-01
Canonicalize identical JSON records created in different key orders.
Identical canonical bytes and digest.
CRYPTO-02
Sign record digest with authorized key; verify with published public key.
Valid signature and correct signer/key ID.
CRYPTO-03
Change one field after signing.
Digest/signature verification fails.
CRYPTO-04
Verify signature after key rotation.
Historical signature validates against key valid at signing time.
CRYPTO-05
Revoke or supersede a record.
Resolver returns current state and retains verifiable history.
CRYPTO-06
Verify blockchain anchor.
Chain ID, contract, transaction receipt, event, block timestamp and digest match expected record.
CRYPTO-07
Attempt unauthorized update.
Operation rejected and audit event recorded.
CRYPTO-08
Submit false physical-world claim with valid signature.
System labels signer attestation separately; signature is not treated as proof claim is true.


Important limitation: a valid signature proves that a key signed particular bytes, subject to key custody and authorization assumptions. A blockchain anchor can show that a commitment was recorded at a point in chain history. Neither proves that a manufacturer assertion, inspection result or physical object is true without a trustworthy evidence process.
8. Physical identification and passport workflow
The Factory01 passport and identity-scanning page are public reference demonstrations of the machine identity/passport concept. The user has clarified that Factory01 is a demonstration and is not live blockchain verification. The scanning demonstration should be described as an interface/workflow demonstration unless and until anti-cloning, cryptographic challenge-response and physical binding are tested.
1. Manufacturer creates/exports robot and component source records.
2. PITN-compatible identity references are assigned or cross-mapped.
3. Product definitions, physical instances and BOM positions are related.
4. Authorized identity/passport view displays permitted records and evidence.
5. Physical label/QR/NFC/RFID is scanned to retrieve the identity reference.
6. Resolver checks record state, source, permissions and evidence.
7. If hardware supports secure challenge-response, authenticate the device separately.
8. Record scan/verification outcome and actor as an auditable event.
A static QR code proves only that the code encodes a reference. It can be copied. Stronger physical authentication requires a secure element or equivalent device-held secret, challenge-response protocol, secure provisioning, revocation and an independently tested threat model.
