Minimum end-to-end demonstration
Register a manufacturer organization with source evidence and verification status unverified.
Register a product definition with manufacturer MPN, revision and sourced specifications.
Register two physical instances with unique serial numbers and separate instance IDs.
Create a robot and versioned configuration/BOM with a defined actuator position.
Install instance A at that position and record evidence and actor.
Remove instance A and install instance B without overwriting instance A's history.
Retrieve the robot's current as-built configuration and the full history for both instances.
Change a source field after signing and demonstrate digest/signature failure.
Export the robot/BOM/identity package to a manufacturer system and round-trip it without losing identifiers, revision, units or provenance.
The test must be run against a named software commit, dependency lockfile, environment, database migration version and configuration. Publish raw test output, test fixture data and known limitations. Do not report a test as passed unless the run is reproducible.
10. Manufacturer pilot and economic validation
The existing PITN economic paper provides a modeled information-cost case. Its modeled $2.5 million annual burden and $572,500 annual savings are hypotheses for pilot measurement, not actual measured savings. A manufacturer pilot should measure baseline and post-implementation workflows using the same definitions and sampling approach.
Metric
Baseline measure
Post-implementation measure
Component identification
Median time to identify correct part, revision and instance.
Same task and sampling rules after PITN workflow.
Document retrieval
Time to find applicable datasheet, drawing, certificate and service record.
Time to retrieve by persistent identity and evidence link.
BOM reconciliation
Time and mismatch rate for a defined BOM sample.
Same BOM size/complexity and mismatch criteria.
Supplier reconciliation
Time to map manufacturer MPN to supplier SKU and source.
Same suppliers and source rules.
Maintenance traceability
Time to identify installed component and service history.
Same task for comparable asset cohort.
Data quality
Duplicate IDs, missing fields, unresolved aliases, stale records.
Same checks after integration.
RFQ/sourcing
Request preparation time, response time and completion rate.
Same sourcing scope; do not equate page click with completed RFQ.


Pre-register scope, sample size, workflow definitions, exclusions and success thresholds.
Use actual manufacturer data under a data-processing agreement and least-privilege access.
Report median and percentile times, error rates, sample counts and confidence/uncertainty; separate one-time integration cost from recurring operating cost.
Record negative results and workflow regressions.
Do not generalize results from one manufacturer or one component class without qualification.
11. Security, privacy and operational controls
Threat model: counterfeit/duplicate identity, copied label, compromised signer key, unauthorized write, malicious supplier data, resolver poisoning, replay, data leakage, tenant crossover, blockchain reorganization, stale cached records.
Authentication: organization and service identities; MFA for privileged human access; short-lived tokens or mutual TLS for machine integrations.
Authorization: least privilege, tenant isolation, role/attribute-based access, approval separation for safety-critical changes.
Integrity: signed events, immutable audit log, deterministic digests, key rotation and revocation.
Privacy: data classification; off-chain confidential records; selective disclosure; retention and deletion policies compatible with legal obligations.
Availability: backups, tested restore, monitoring, rate limiting, queue retry/dead-letter handling and disaster recovery objectives.
Safety boundary: PITN identity data is not a safety controller and must not directly authorize motion or replace functional-safety engineering.
12. Standards and semantic alignment
The implementation should evaluate relevant standards and specifications rather than claim blanket compliance. Candidate areas for technical mapping include ISO 8000 data quality, ISO 9001 quality-management records, IEC 62443 industrial cybersecurity, ISO/IEC 27001 information security management, IEC 61508/ISO 13849 functional safety boundaries, OPC UA information modelling, AutomationML, GS1 identifiers where applicable, W3C Verifiable Credentials and DID specifications where the chosen identity model actually conforms, JSON Schema 2020-12, OpenAPI 3.1 and RFC 8785 JSON canonicalization. Applicability and edition must be assessed by the manufacturer and independent reviewer.
The namespace model is PITN's blockchain-based architecture. Any claim of conformance to a particular DID method, VC data model, GS1 scheme, OPC UA companion specification or industrial standard must be supported by a documented mapping and conformance test.
13. Limitations and unresolved design decisions
Area
Current limitation / decision required
Production software
Repository documents include a proposed/untested API specification and code-concept text. A runnable, versioned service, dependencies, CI and test report must be identified.
Database
SQLite is a proposed prototype option. Production DB choice, migrations, constraints, backup, restore and tenancy are not evidenced by the cited documents.
Namespace registration
Dashboard and user-supplied registrations are evidence supplied by the owner. Independent per-TLD chain verification still requires registry/transaction/resolver artifacts.
Revenue
Reseller and royalty activation evidence supports a commercial mechanism, not realized revenue.
Passport
Factory01 is a demonstration; not live blockchain verification.
Physical authentication
Scanning a label/reference does not prove the label is original or the object authentic. Secure hardware challenge-response requires implementation and test.
Interoperability
ERP/PLM/MES integration architecture is specified here; live external system exchanges have not been established by reviewed evidence.
Cryptography
Signature, digest, key lifecycle, anchoring and revocation procedures are proposed here and require implementation/independent tests.
Compatibility
Data model supports assessments; validated engineering compatibility rules and evidence are still required.
Supplier/RFQ
Marketplace shows RFQ entry points; successful submission, routing, supplier response and transaction lifecycle need operational evidence.
Economic pilot
Published economic figures are modeled; measured manufacturer outcomes remain outstanding.
Independent review
No independent technical/security review is included in the reviewed evidence.


14. Peer-to-peer technical review protocol
Reviewers should evaluate the architecture against the evidence and implementation artifacts, not against marketing statements. The review should produce a signed or attributable report with scope, versions, methods, findings, severity, reproducibility and limitations.
Review package item
Reviewer action
Architecture and entity model
Check identity separation, cardinality, immutable identifiers, versioning and lifecycle semantics.
Namespace registry/crosswalk
Check 27-to-34 mapping, definitions, governance, registration references and status reconciliation.
JSON Schema and test vectors
Validate positive/negative records and schema evolution.
Repository source and commit
Inspect actual executable source, dependencies, build steps, tests and deployment manifests.
Passport/scan demonstrations
Reproduce visible workflows and distinguish UI behavior from backend, cryptographic and physical authentication.
API/OpenAPI and adapters
Run requests against a test environment; validate auth, errors, idempotency and mapping fidelity.
Cryptographic evidence
Independently recompute digests, verify signatures, inspect key controls and chain anchors.
Security and privacy
Review threat model, access controls, tenant boundaries, logging and data minimization.
Economic pilot
Reproduce baseline/post calculations and evaluate sampling, confounders and costs.


Recommended reviewer outputs: (1) architecture review, (2) schema/conformance report, (3) implementation and test report, (4) security review, (5) standards mapping, and (6) economic-methodology review. Findings should be classified Critical / High / Medium / Low / Informational, with remediation owner and retest evidence.
15. Acceptance criteria for a manufacturer-ready implementation
Gate
Minimum acceptance condition
Status in this document
A — Identity schema
Versioned schema, data dictionary, valid/invalid examples and product/instance distinction.
Specified; JSON Schema companion supplied.
B — Namespace registry
34 definitions, original-27 crosswalk, governance/versioning and per-TLD registration evidence.
Crosswalk supplied; registry governance specified; on-chain proof incomplete.
C — Passport / identity UX
Manufacturer can resolve identity and display authorized passport fields.
Public demos supplied; backend and security boundary require test.
D — Runnable core service
Buildable API, persistent database, migrations, auth and automated tests.
Not established by reviewed docs; development/verification required.
E — BOM/lifecycle
Versioned BOM, install/remove/replace history and traceability query pass.
Data model documented; end-to-end test required.
F — Interoperability
At least one ERP/PLM/MES or supplier round-trip passes with reconciliation.
Specified; not yet evidenced.
G — Cryptographic verification
Signature, tamper detection, revocation and chain anchor independently pass.
Specified; not yet evidenced.
H — Security
Threat model, access controls, audit and independent security assessment.
Requirements specified; assessment outstanding.
I — Pilot economics
Measured baseline/post results and full cost accounting.
Modeled case exists; actual pilot outstanding.
J — Independent review
External review report and closed critical/high findings.
Outstanding.


Manufacturer implementation readiness is therefore defined as an architecture and contract package that can be implemented in a manufacturer's network, plus a set of conformance gates. This document supplies the architecture, schema contract, mapping and test protocol. It does not falsely certify that all gates have already passed.
