17. Final technical position
PITN has an established architecture proposition, a documented original 27-namespace robotics BOM taxonomy, a user-supplied expanded 34-TLD management inventory, reported Polygon lifetime domain examples, a public ROBOPARTS component/RFQ interface, public identity/passport demonstrations, and repository documentation defining component/product identity, physical instances, BOM relationships, lifecycle events, evidence and blockchain namespace architecture.
The evidence supports presenting PITN as a developed and demonstrable identity-and-namespace architecture with a manufacturer implementation pathway. It does not support representing every planned API, integration, cryptographic verification process, production database, economic outcome or independent certification as complete.
A manufacturer can implement the architecture within its own network by adopting the canonical identity schema, mapping its existing identifiers, linking its product and physical-instance records, relating instances to versioned BOM/configuration positions, and exchanging authorized references with its ERP/PLM/MES and supplier systems. The manufacturer's systems remain authoritative for their source records. PITN provides the persistent identity, namespace and relationship framework.
The next engineering work is not to restart the architecture. It is to formalize and version the schema, implement the conformance tests, establish the namespace registry evidence, build/verify the runnable service and adapters, execute cryptographic and interoperability tests, and publish the results—including failures and limitations.
CONNECT THE PHYSICAL COMPONENT TO THE INFORMATION THAT ALREADY DESCRIBES IT.
